      "writtenOn": "2017-10-17T21:19:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Now that you\u0027ve had me shorten the code, I\u0027ll update the comment to match. Something more like:\n\n// If the existence of B in any input list implies the existence of C later in that input list,\n// Then\n//   After concatenating all lists, the final instance of C will have come from the same\n//     original list as the final instance of B\n//   So the final instance of C will be later in the concatenation than the final instance of B\n//   So, keeping only the final instance of B and of C ensures that B is earlier in the output\n//     list than C\n// Else\n//   # B is not always followed by C\n//   This either means that B does not depend on C, or that there exists an A that depends on\n//     B but that does not declare a transitive dependency on C\n//   If B does not depend on C, then B need not precede C in the output list\n//   If A depends on B but does not declare a transitive dependency on C\n//   Then\n//     This case isn\u0027t expected to happen often, if at all.\n//     It\u0027s not clear what the desired behavior is in this case.\n//     For now we have a unit test confirming that changes in the behavior of this case are intentional\n//   Endif\n// Endif",
      "parentUuid": "6c23add4_25db5c3b",
      "revId": "d7b5f43d29e4be6a44b30a7cad7a3f29ce09defd",
